{
  "question": "What causes the symptoms of inflammation?",
  "answer_structure": {
    "answer_type": "single",
    "answer": "cause",
    "effect": "symptoms of inflammation (redness, swelling, heat, and pain)",
    "cause": "increased blood flow into tissue, eicosanoids, and cytokines",
    "mechanism": "Inflammation is triggered by the immune system in response to infection or injury. The symptoms are caused by increased blood flow due to vasodilation, which is mediated by eicosanoids like prostaglandins. Cytokines, including interleukins, chemokines, and interferons, are released by injured or infected cells. These chemicals recruit immune cells to the site of infection, promote communication between immune cells, and support the healing process."
  },
  "context_passage": "Inflammation is one of the first responses of the immune system to infection. The symptoms of inflammation are redness, swelling, heat, and pain, which are caused by increased blood flow into tissue. Inflammation is produced by eicosanoids and cytokines, which are released by injured or infected cells. Eicosanoids include prostaglandins that produce fever and the dilation of blood vessels associated with inflammation, and leukotrienes that attract certain white blood cells (leukocytes). Common cytokines include interleukins that are responsible for communication between white blood cells; chemokines that promote chemotaxis; and interferons that have anti-viral effects, such as shutting down protein synthesis in the host cell. Growth factors and cytotoxic factors may also be released. These cytokines and other chemicals recruit immune cells to the site of infection and promote healing of any damaged tissue following the removal of pathogens."
}

