    "description_domain": "The Pauli exclusion principle manifests in the macro world as the rigidity of solid matter. This is because electrons, being fermions, cannot occupy the same quantum state, leading to a resistance against being compressed. This resistance is experienced macroscopically as the structural rigidity of solids."
  },
  "context_passage": "It is a common misconception to ascribe the stiffness and rigidity of solid matter to the repulsion of like charges under the influence of the electromagnetic force. However, these characteristics actually result from the Pauli exclusion principle.[citation needed] Since electrons are fermions, they cannot occupy the same quantum mechanical state as other electrons. When the electrons in a material are densely packed together, there are not enough lower energy quantum mechanical states for them all, so some of them must be in higher energy states. This means that it takes energy to pack them together. While this effect is manifested macroscopically as a structural force, it is technically only the result of the existence of a finite set of electron states."
